// Goddard Caddis: the G&H Sedge of John Goddard and Cliff Henry, in its modern dressing.

id goddard-caddis
name "Goddard Caddis"
aliases "G&H Sedge" "Goddard Sedge"
type dry
imitates caddis
difficulty advanced
species trout grayling
season june july august september
origin originator="John Goddard and Cliff Henry" region="England"
description "A body of spun and clipped deer hair trimmed to a sedge's tent-shaped wing, and a brown hackle at the front."
history """
    Developed by John Goddard and Cliff Henry in England; G&H are their initials. The Feather
    Bender takes the original dressing from a 1977 reprint of Goddard's Trout Flies of
    Still-Water (1969): a belly of dark green seal's fur, two rusty dun hackles and their
    stripped stems as antennae. Hans Weilenmann gives the belly as olive. Today it is
    mostly tied with a hard-packed hair body and no belly, and is better known as the Goddard
    Caddis; posters on Fly Anglers Online, Weilenmann among them, credit that name to the
    American tyer Andre Puyans.
    """
fishing """
    Packed deer hair makes it very buoyant, even in rough water, so it can be skated,
    twitched or pulled to leave a wake, and that movement is often what draws a rise. Global
    FlyFisher notes that it is easy to see in the last light of a big hatch, but finds it
    too clumsy for very selective trout.
    """
sources {
    "The Feather Bender: Fly Tying Course #19, The G & H Sedge (Barry Ord Clarke)" url="https://thefeatherbender.com/2013/08/23/fly-tying-course-19-the-g-h-sedge/"
    "Orvis Learning Center: Goddard Caddis (video)" url="https://howtoflyfish.orvis.com/fly-tying-videos/dry-flies/731-goddard-caddis"
    "Global FlyFisher: Goddard Caddis" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/goddard-caddis-4"
    "Fly Anglers Online: Goddard Caddis SBS (Scott P., with comments by Hans Weilenmann)" url="https://www.flyanglersonline.com/t/goddard-caddis-sbs/50306"
    "Fly Anglers Online: Thin Goddard Caddis (forum thread)" url="https://www.flyanglersonline.com/t/thin-goddard-caddis/54085"
    "Medlar Press: John Goddard" url="https://medlarpress.com/Authors/john-goddard.html"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (npm run refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://i0.wp.com/thefeatherbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/01/Goddard-Caddis-G-H-Sedge.jpg?resize=1300%2C768&ssl=1" page="https://thefeatherbender.com/2013/08/23/fly-tying-course-19-the-g-h-sedge/" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise"
    "https://www.fish4flies.com/img/flies/Large/1497-1497%202.jpg" page="https://www.fish4flies.com/Dry/Sedge/Deer-Hair-Sedge" license="All rights reserved; reference only" flip=#true note="Side view"
}

hook dry-standard size=12
thread brown size="8/0"

materials {
    body deer-hair
    hackle cock-hackle color=brown fibre=1.0
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.6
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Assumption: the first bunch's tips left trailing past the bend, about a third of the shank.
    spin body to=0.72 collar=0.7 note="Comb out the underfur; pack each bunch back hard against the last."
    trim body leave=0.6 shape=wedge note="Flat underneath at the depth of the eye, the top rising towards the rear, the sides flaring back."
    tie-in hackle note="Slightly undersized."
    wrap-thread to=0.92
    wind hackle to=0.9 turns=5 note="A couple of turns back into the hair, then forward."
    tie-off hackle
    whip-finish
}
